Revision history for agent-construction-kit

{{$NEXT}}
    - Add claude-with, the launcher behind every starter: it loads a provider
      definition, finds the key, sets the environment and starts Claude Code.
      Definitions ship for MiniMax, Moonshot AI Kimi, Ollama Cloud, Fireworks
      AI and OpenAI through claude-code-proxy.
    - Add ack-wrappers, which creates, tests and removes the starters
      (claude_with_<name>), opens the key file in an editor, changes provider
      values and adds starters for local model servers.
    - Add ack-proxy, which reports on the claude-code-proxy behind the openai
      provider, lists its models, checks the model slots and sets up a user
      service on Linux.
    - Add ack-doctor, a read-only check of the machine: Claude Code, git, the
      plugins agent teams build on, optional tools and the installed starters.
    - The kit works through three manual commands, which Claude never starts on
      its own: /agent-machine, /agent-setup and /agent-tune (in full
      /agent-construction-kit:agent-machine and so on). There is no single
      skill and no entry question any more. /agent-machine sets up a machine
      in plain questions (prerequisites, a starter per provider, OpenAI through
      the proxy, local models) and ends by offering /agent-setup; its steps
      are in references/machine.md of that command. A preset is loaded by
      /agent-setup and /agent-machine.
    - Find claude-code-proxy in ~/.local/bin and ~/bin when it is not on PATH
      (or at ACK_PROXY_BIN); ack-proxy status and ack-doctor say so, and the
      sign-in hints then carry the full path.
    - ack-proxy check-slots checks the small-model slot too.
    - ack-wrappers new-local tells you to pass --replace when the definition
      is its own; ack-wrappers --help prints the whole header.
    - ack-wrappers install reports "path pending" instead of "path missing"
      once path-fix has added the directory to the shell profile.
    - Build an agent team for a project with /agent-setup: it reads the
      folder, asks what it could not read, shows a plan, and after a yes writes
      the agents, the project skill, the rules, the board and the CLAUDE.md
      section, checks the result and commits it.
    - Add ack-repo: inspect reads a folder and changes nothing (git, branch,
      remotes, tests, sibling projects, an existing team, accounts of git
      services), init creates the repository, disk-remote puts a copy on a
      second drive. A folder inside another repository, or below a home
      folder kept in git, is reported as such and gets a repository of its
      own with init --own; init and disk-remote never run on the home folder,
      and disk-remote only on a repository of its own. A .git that git
      refuses to read (dubious ownership, a broken link, an empty folder) is
      reported with its reason, and init then writes nothing.
    - Add ack-team check, which verifies a team: agent files, the skills they
      name, rules, settings, board, CLAUDE.md, git (with the reason when git
      refuses the repository). The agent, skill, hook and settings findings are
      briefing's (briefing doctor --tsv, from the briefing command on PATH, the
      plugin's own starter); ack-team parses no JSON itself and, when no
      briefing answers, reports one missing line.
    - One install of agent-construction-kit brings skilletor and briefing
      too: the plugin declares them as dependencies.
    - Team setup finds installed and trusted source skills before authoring,
      explains that managed skills are edited at their source then synced, and
      offers an optional local or private collection without publishing it.
    - Build teams from planned as well as existing work, selecting only the
      capabilities each task needs. Each team has one OWNERS map, and
      ack-team owner resolves a path's assigned owner before work is handed out.
    - Add /agent-tune: a manual, project-scoped, read-only
      review of past assignments that reports evidence, uncertainty and
      proposed routing changes without editing, publishing or installing.
    - A board without installing anything: BOARD.md, a table per column. karr
      is offered in the board question, recommended for a family of projects,
      and installed after a yes.
    - A preset (a skill with an answer sheet) can fix answers and add skills
      per role; the team build installs the sources, bundles and skills it
      names, not its starters: the hand-over points to /agent-machine for them.
    - Teams for folders without code: a reading check instead of a test
      command, no test-writer.
    - ack-doctor checks that skilletor and briefing run and prints their
      versions; it asks for neither Node.js nor Python 3.
    - ack-doctor and ack-team advise installing the program a failing
      skilletor or briefing names as missing (Node.js, perl) instead of
      updating the plugin; a plugin enabled only in a project scope is not
      taken for enabled everywhere, and the advice says where to enable it.
    - ack-doctor and ack-team say what to install when briefing cannot run
      because the perl on PATH is missing ("Install perl") or does not load
      briefing's Perl modules (cpanm --installdeps on the plugin folder), instead
      of advising a plugin update that does not help.
    - ack-team check no longer says "hook ok" on a machine where briefing's
      hook cannot run: the hook runs on Python 3 (python3, then python; on
      Windows also py), so with none found the hook line is "missing" and says
      to install Python 3 and start Claude Code again.
    - Team texts follow the roles the plan chose: without a reviewer the main
      agent checks before the commit, a card names a result and its delegated
      steps sit below it, the reviewer reads git status and git diff HEAD, and
      read-only roles are described as changing nothing, not as unable to write.
    - ack-team check looks at OWNERS too: ok for a valid map, missing for one
      ack-team owner would refuse, a warning without a map (teams made before
      it have none).
    - ack-wrappers test no longer hangs when claude ignores the TERM sent at
      its timeout: after two seconds it gets KILL.
    - In an admin folder, ack-team check reads AREAS (folder, kind, board per
      tab-separated row) and prints an areas line per row: ok when the folder
      is a git repository with agents and the board the row names, missing
      otherwise, for a malformed table, or for shape=hub without AREAS, and a
      warning when the table has no row.
    - ack-wrappers install records the PID of the run that holds the data
      lock: a lock whose holder is gone is taken at once, one held by a live
      install is never taken; after ACK_LOCK_WAIT seconds (default 60) the
      second install stops and names the lock.
    - The bounded calls of ack-doctor, ack-team and ack-proxy stop what a
      timed-out or interrupted command started, not only the command itself
      (its process group; on Git for Windows the command alone).
    - ack-proxy and the openai starter recognise claude-code-proxy by its
      /healthz answer: another program on the proxy port ends with exit 5 and
      a message to pick a free port, and is never stopped; ack-proxy status
      shows it as not the proxy.
    - ack-repo disk-remote carries branches, tags and karr's cards
      (refs/karr) to the copy, on creation and on every later git push <name>;
      it reports only what was pushed, and running it again on an older copy
      adds the missing push lines.
    - /agent-tune: every proposal to split a role names the failure, knowledge
      gap, context conflict or bottleneck it fixes and the hand-over it costs;
      a split that only redistributes work is shown as that.
    - A team build with several presets asks when two of them set one key to
      different values (or one source alias to different addresses), naming
      both presets; list keys combine, and nothing is switched silently.
    - Teams for folders without code derive the check from the result first
      (every document there, sums and units agree, each claim sourced, open
      exceptions shown), form points after, and a command where one measures.
    - The README presents the product for a first-time reader (pitch, the first
      team, a Status section) and states the current limits: skilletor needs
      Node.js 18 or newer and briefing's hooks need Python 3 until their
      standalone binaries are switched on. The machine and team references say
      the same.
    - Add ack-survey [DIR]: reports what lies below the home folder by names
      only (platform, hub, projects with their last commit date and whether
      they have a team, families, signals), and says "partial" when the time
      bound ACK_SURVEY_WAIT (default 30 seconds) cuts it short. It opens no
      file below DIR and changes nothing.
    - ack-repo inspect names the home folder with a home line and reads
      nothing further there.
    - /agent-setup typed in the home folder runs hub mode: the survey with its
      permission question, the questions in three blocks (work environment,
      work, customers), the plan, then the build of the areas and the admin
      with its AREAS table. The admin looks after this computer, on every
      platform.
    - The README names the home start first: /agent-setup builds from two
      starts, the new section "Start in your home folder" walks through it,
      the roadmap marks the admin done, and the commands list has an
      ack-survey entry.
    - The README says what the home start has proven: one real run on Linux,
      with the macOS and Windows runs still open. The admin holds a card for
      this computer on every platform; its subagents for the computer are on
      the roadmap, listed once under the kind machine.
